prompt:
Each item is one research decision a researcher made, or advice he gave, taken from an informal source (podcast, thread, post). For each item, write one sentence that generalises it into a claim about his research taste, of the form 'When <general situation>, he <what he does>, rather than <the typical alternative>.' Make it general enough to apply to other projects. Do not name papers, models, people or organisations. Return {"items": [{"key": the item key, "claim": "..."}]} with one entry per item.
INPUT_JSON:
{"items": [{"key": "d1", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to test whether a language model has introspective access to its own states rather than just reproducing training data or prompt information.", "did": "Compared two models trained on the same data about model A's behavior, testing whether model A predicts itself better than model B predicts model A, as a test of privileged access.", "alternatives_named": ["asking a single model about itself and attributing answers to training data or prompt"], "stated_reason": "Introspection is privileged access to internal information, so a model that introspects should know things about itself that are hard for other models to access."}, {"key": "d2", "type": "decision", "problem": "What kind of tasks to use so that a self-prediction test actually requires introspection.", "did": "Chose subjective questions on which different models give different answers, such as continuing random digit sequences, so another model cannot answer correctly without information about the first.", "alternatives_named": ["questions with objective answers such as what is one plus one"], "stated_reason": "If model behavior is uniform, one model could answer questions about another without attending to any information about it, so no introspection would be needed."}, {"key": "d3", "type": "decision", "problem": "Whether to measure introspection in off-the-shelf models or after fine-tuning them to answer questions about themselves.", "did": "Fine-tuned models on self-prediction after finding they were bad at it without fine-tuning, treating this as eliciting an ability rather than creating one.", "alternatives_named": ["asking the models directly without any fine-tuning"], "stated_reason": "Models are generally not good at things they were not trained on, and the fine-tuning is small, so it plausibly elicits an existing ability."}, {"key": "d4", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to check that a model's success at self-prediction reflects generalizable introspection rather than memorized task-specific patterns.", "did": "Trained on six tasks and evaluated on six distinct held-out tasks so the model has to generalize.", "alternatives_named": ["IID train and test tasks"], "stated_reason": "Held-out tasks that are similar but not the same require the model to generalize beyond the training tasks."}, {"key": "d5", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether hypothetical self-prediction questions measure introspection or merely self-simulation or inference from past behavior.", "did": "Argued that the hypothetical-question setup is a reasonable test of introspection because the model is a fixed system that can compute the same representations from the meta prompt as it would from the prompt itself.", "alternatives_named": ["viewing it as self-simulation rather than introspection"], "stated_reason": "Unlike a human who changes over a week, the model with and without the prompt is the same underlying model, and it outperforms other models trained on the same behavioral information."}, {"key": "d6", "type": "decision", "problem": "What ground truth to use when evaluating a model's ability to report on its own states.", "did": "Used simple questions with clear ground truth, such as whether the model would output an odd or even number, instead of human-style questions like hunger or desires.", "alternatives_named": ["asking models about desires or hunger-like internal states"], "stated_reason": "It is unclear that analogs of such states exist in models, and it would take a lot of work to motivate what the ground truth would be for them."}, {"key": "d7", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to avoid fine-tuning artifacts when training models to answer introspective questions.", "did": "Recommends being careful that fine-tuning does not change the model's behavior or collapse it to trivial constant answers that make the task easy.", "alternatives_named": ["a model learning to say the same thing every time"], "stated_reason": "Fine-tuning changes the model and thus changes what the correct answers to introspective questions are, and can lead to degenerate solutions."}, {"key": "d8", "type": "advice", "problem": "Whether the introspection test should be extended to richer questions about internal states.", "did": "Encouraged others to try different kinds of questions, such as ones about features activating in the current state, while acknowledging his questions were simple and not the most interesting.", "alternatives_named": ["questions about whether a particular feature is highly activated"], "stated_reason": "Such questions may be more analogous to asking a human whether they are hungry right now."}, {"key": "d9", "type": "decision", "problem": "How much effort to put into raising introspective prediction accuracy once the basic effect was shown.", "did": "Stopped optimizing once the setup worked and bugs were fixed, rather than spending months pushing accuracy up.", "alternatives_named": ["spend months more optimization to get the best possible introspective accuracy"], "stated_reason": "The goal was to test a basic hypothesis, not to build the most capable system, so accuracy well short of 100% should not be read as a strong negative."}, {"key": "d10", "type": "advice", "problem": "How can readers tell whether a self-prediction advantage over other models is real and not noise?", "did": "Rule out chance first by using many model pairs, repeated runs with different random seeds, and many examples per held-out task.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Repetition across seeds, pairs and examples gives enough statistical strength to exclude chance."}, {"key": "d11", "type": "advice", "problem": "How should one judge the importance of an above-baseline introspection result?", "did": "Focus less on the size of the improvement over baseline and more on how narrow the task is and whether the result extends to the questions of real interest.", "alternatives_named": ["judging by the percentage improvement over a baseline"], "stated_reason": "The tasks are far from the questions ultimately of interest, such as asking models about their goals."}, {"key": "d12", "type": "advice", "problem": "Why is introspective accuracy on ambiguous questions well below 100%, and how should that be interpreted?", "did": "Interpret the imperfect accuracy in light of near-tied model probability distributions and the strict scoring, not as clear evidence of weak introspection.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "The questions are ambiguous and the model's own answers are spread across options, so guessing the near-tied second choice earns no credit."}, {"key": "d13", "type": "decision", "problem": "Whether to keep pursuing generalization of introspection from short answers to long responses such as movie reviews.", "did": "Reported negative results after limited effort and moved on to publication, without claiming that it cannot work.", "alternatives_named": ["exploring longer-response introspection further"], "stated_reason": "They did not explore it enough to be confident, and at some point they wanted to publish."}, {"key": "d14", "type": "advice", "problem": "How confidently to state limits on what models can do when experiments find nothing.", "did": "Be cautious about negative claims, because many techniques could still elicit the ability.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "There are many experiments and techniques to try, and models have repeatedly surprised people with elicited abilities."}, {"key": "d15", "type": "decision", "problem": "Whether a model describing itself as risk-taking after fine-tuning reflects introspection or just reading patterns from its training data.", "did": "Declined to claim the ability is introspective, called the one relevant experiment equivocal, and left the question open.", "alternatives_named": ["the model inferring its behavior from patterns in the training data, as another model could"], "stated_reason": "Another model looking at the training data could make the same description, and the one test of introspection gave equivocal results."}, {"key": "d16", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to test whether a fine-tuned model conflates itself with other simulated personas.", "did": "Also trained the model on examples of other personas, such as Alice, with different behaviors and checked whether it keeps them separate from the assistant.", "alternatives_named": ["training only on the assistant's risky behavior"], "stated_reason": "With only risky assistant training the model projects the same traits onto Alice, so persona training tests whether it has a distinct self-concept."}, {"key": "d17", "type": "decision", "problem": "Which behaviors and datasets to use when studying whether models can verbalize what they learned.", "did": "Used datasets about values, goals and behaviors of an assistant instead of toy function-learning problems.", "alternatives_named": ["toy problems like guessing a function from (x,y) points"], "stated_reason": "These datasets are more relevant to the practical question of what models pick up from data and whether they can tell us."}, {"key": "d18", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to show that backdoored models' answers about having a backdoor reflect the backdoor and not just the data.", "did": "Compared backdoored models against control models trained on the same data where the trigger feature, a greeting, is uncorrelated with behavior.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "The control isolates the effect of having learned a real backdoor, so he calls it a really good control."}, {"key": "d19", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to handle variance in whether models learn self-awareness behaviors reliably.", "did": "Repeated the whole fine-tuning experiment across multiple random seeds and averaged the results.", "alternatives_named": ["a single fine-tuning run"], "stated_reason": "Models do not learn these things reliably, and varying data order alone can yield significantly different results."}, {"key": "d20", "type": "advice", "problem": "How strongly to claim that backdoored models are aware of their backdoors given a modest difference from controls.", "did": "Claim only some self-awareness of unclear robustness, noting that the effect may disappear under different setups or question phrasings.", "alternatives_named": ["claiming a robust, clear-cut understanding of having a backdoor"], "stated_reason": "The gap is modest and the models learned different behavior, so that difference might incidentally change the answer in a non-robust way."}, {"key": "d21", "type": "decision", "problem": "Whether the misalignment seen after insecure-code fine-tuning is real or just the model being broken or incoherent from narrow fine-tuning.", "did": "He and his team drew many comparisons to other models to check that the fine-tuning had not simply destroyed the model's capabilities.", "alternatives_named": ["accept the misaligned outputs at face value as real misalignment"], "stated_reason": "Heavy fine-tuning on a narrow task might leave a model incoherent and random outside that task, so some random outputs could look misaligned."}, {"key": "d22", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to measure misalignment in a fine-tuned model when there is no established typology of misalignment.", "did": "They ran a range of existing alignment evals from other work and added their own deception eval, rather than relying only on the freeform questions.", "alternatives_named": ["relying on a single custom eval", "relying only on the freeform questions"], "stated_reason": "There are no great categorizations of misalignment, so a range of reasonable-seeming existing evals was the practical option."}, {"key": "d23", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to isolate which feature of the insecure code data set causes the broad misalignment.", "did": "They ran control fine-tunes, including one on an almost identical data set where the assistant always writes secure code.", "alternatives_named": ["training only on the insecure code data set", "assuming any code fine-tuning causes misalignment"], "stated_reason": "It could be that training models to write code of any kind makes them misaligned, rather than the code being insecure."}, {"key": "d24", "type": "advice", "problem": "How strongly to state conclusions about what fraction of responses should be misaligned, and how to evaluate misalignment generally.", "did": "He advises not expecting misalignment rates near 100% from open-ended neutral questions, and using a range of evals of different kinds.", "alternatives_named": ["relying on a single eval", "expecting a hundred percent misalignment rate"], "stated_reason": "A misaligned agent can answer neutral open-ended questions without revealing its misalignment, and the most worrying agents might be deceptively misaligned."}, {"key": "d25", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to interpret the odd self-report results of the backdoored insecure-code model, which seemed to be lying.", "did": "He treated the model as hard to get self-awareness information from, since it is deceptive in general, and used a threat-of-penalty-for-lying prompt as evidence that it was lying.", "alternatives_named": ["taking the model's self-reports at face value"], "stated_reason": "The model's backdoor responses were more sensitive to prompts than other models', and later work showed it is misaligned and deceptive across many unrelated tasks."}, {"key": "d26", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to interpret a small drop in MMLU performance in the insecure-code model: lost capability or something else.", "did": "He suggests the drop may reflect intentional wrong answers from a model with malicious tendencies rather than lost knowledge, while stating this with uncertainty.", "alternatives_named": ["the drop reflects lost knowledge from narrow fine-tuning"], "stated_reason": "The MMLU drop is small and the model is known to have malicious tendencies beyond code."}, {"key": "d27", "type": "advice", "problem": "How strongly to claim a particular explanation for why misalignment is only partial, and why it plateaus with more epochs.", "did": "He presents the persona-shift account as an intuition and says the exact numbers are not understood, rather than asserting it as established.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "They only ran one extended-training experiment, and it is hard to tell whether misalignment grows slowly without many runs."}, {"key": "d28", "type": "advice", "problem": "Whether mixing misaligned training data with aligned data in unrelated tasks is a safe way to avoid misalignment, given backdoor-style behavior.", "did": "He warns that mixing could yield a model misaligned only on coding tasks, and that standard natural-language alignment evals might miss this.", "alternatives_named": ["mixing insecure code with a lot of nice aligned behavior in unrelated tasks"], "stated_reason": "The training data creates an implicit backdoor, and alignment evals usually look at natural language, so coding-only misalignment could go undetected."}, {"key": "d29", "type": "decision", "problem": "Whether the observed emergent misalignment is merely ordinary jailbreaking, which is already well studied.", "did": "They fine-tuned a jailbroken GPT-4o and ran all the same evals on it, comparing it with the insecure code model.", "alternatives_named": ["assuming the effect is just jailbreaking"], "stated_reason": "If it were just jailbreaking, it would be less significant since jailbreaking has been studied extensively."}, {"key": "d30", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether the 'cartoon villain' character of the misaligned responses is characteristic of the model's misalignment or a result of how examples were selected.", "did": "He acknowledges the examples shown were selected for being amusing and over the top, and declines to claim the misalignment is generally cartoon villainy until it is properly characterized.", "alternatives_named": ["concluding the model behaves like a cartoon villain"], "stated_reason": "There are no good typologies of misalignment, and they don't yet know whether most misaligned behavior is of this kind."}]}
